# Code signing certificates used to sign malware, published by abuse.ch.
#
# WHAT THIS MAPS, AND WHY IT IS NOT A HASH
# The CSCB carries a thumbprint column, which is the hash OF THE CERTIFICATE.
# It is not the hash of any file, so mapping it to sha256 would build hundreds of
# definitions that load cleanly and can never match anything. That is the exact
# failure the retired definition format shipped with & it is not repeated here.
# The serial_number column is different. A serial number really is stored inside a
# signed executable, as raw bytes in the certificate table, so it is decoded from
# hex and matched as a byte signature against file content.
#
# Confidence is medium. A blocklisted serial in a file is strong evidence but the
# match is a byte sequence rather than a whole file hash, so it reports Suspicious.
#
# NOT redistributed. The abuse.ch terms make the data free under fair use
# principles while noting that commercial or for profit use may require a paid
# subscription. HRConvert2 is GPLv3 & is deployed commercially, so baking this into
# a published artefact would hand downstream operators an obligation they never
# agreed to. Build it at home instead.
url          = https://bazaar.abuse.ch/export/csv/cscb/
file         = abusech-cscb.csv
format       = csv
comment      = #
kind         = data
value        = column 2
transform    = hexbytes
label        = column 9
labelprefix  = Malware.SignedCert.
category     = Malware
confidence   = medium
license      = abuse.ch Terms of Use, free under fair use principles
attribution  = Data from abuse.ch MalwareBazaar, https://bazaar.abuse.ch
redistribute = no
